lie in (0, 100).}

\item{threads}{requested OpenMP threads; \code{0} (default) enables
automatic threading. A positive request bypasses automatic row-count,
FE-shape, and \code{max_threads} heuristics and is clamped only to the
logical processors/OpenMP runtime limit visible to the process. A serial
build fails explicitly for requests above one.}

\item{default_threads}{default thread count applied when automatic
threading is enabled; \code{0} lets the backend decide (Stata
\code{defaultthreads()}).}

\item{max_threads}{maximum thread count when automatic threading is
enabled; \code{0} means no explicit cap (Stata \code{maxthreads()}).}

\item{min_parallel_rows}{minimum number of rows before parallel
absorption is enabled; default \code{20000} (Stata
\code{minparallelrows()}).}

\item{target_rows_per_thread}{rows per thread used to size the thread pool
under automatic threading (Stata \code{targetrowsperthread()}). The
default \code{0} is automatic: one thread per 25,000 rows when fixed
effects are absorbed with \code{save_fe = FALSE}, within the problem-size
cap (one thread below 100,000 rows, at most 8 below 250,000 and at most 16
otherwise), and one thread per 500,000 rows with \code{save_fe = TRUE} or
without fixed effects. A positive value applies that fixed number of rows
per thread on every path; \code{500000} restores the automatic rule of
version 2.30.1. Setting \code{XHDFE_AUTO_ROWS_PER_THREAD} to a positive
integer before the first fit replaces the 25,000 for the session. On Linux
and macOS the automatic teams of one user's simultaneous fits share the
machine: such a fit takes the smallest of its automatic team, the logical
processors divided by the number of fits running and the processors not
taken by the other fits' absorptions, never fewer threads than
\code{target_rows_per_thread = 500000} would give. A positive
\code{threads} is never reduced. A lowered team reports
\code{thread_limit_code} 6 and \code{thread_limit_reason}
\code{"auto_team_coordination"}. \code{XHDFE_AUTO_TEAM_COORDINATION=0}
before the first fit turns this off.}

\item{backend}{the compute backend: \code{"default"} (follow the
\code{XHDFE_GPU_BACKEND} environment variable if set, otherwise CPU),
\code{"cpu"} (force the CPU path), \code{"cuda"} (request the CUDA GPU
absorber), or \code{"metal"} (reserved for Metal builds; errors where
unavailable). Requesting \code{"cuda"} or \code{"metal"} is
\emph{fail-closed}: if the GPU is not effectively used the fit stops
with an error, mirroring the Stata plugin's error 498 contract. With
\code{weighted_cpu_route = TRUE}, eligible fits run on the CPU and report
\code{gpu_status_code} 7. See the \emph{GPU backends} section for the rule, the
build recipe and the diagnostics to check.}

\item{na.action}{\code{"omit"} (default): rows with missing values in any
estimation variable (outcome, regressors, instruments, fixed-effect
ids, slope variables, clusters, weights, group/individual ids) are
dropped before estimation, and full-length outputs (residuals,
prediction caches) are \code{NA} on those rows. \code{"fail"}: raise an
error if any such missing value is present.}

\item{reduced_pcg}{the reduced-system tier for this fit (see
\code{absorption_method}): \code{TRUE} on, \code{FALSE} off, \code{NULL}
(default) the process default, which is the environment variable
\code{XHDFE_REDUCED_PCG} (read once per process; \code{0} turns the tier
off) and otherwise on. It allows fits with and without the tier in the
same R session. A fit with the tier switched off reports
\code{reduced_pcg_status} 0 and \code{reduced_pcg_reason} \code{"off"},
and gives the results of the tier switched off for the whole process.}

\item{weighted_cpu_route}{Logical scalar, default \code{FALSE}. With
\code{TRUE}, request CPU for eligible weighted CUDA fits and report
\code{gpu_status_code} 7. See \emph{GPU backends}.}

\item{reduced_pcg_cuda}{the experimental CUDA variant of the reduced-system
tier for this fit, which acts only on a fit on CUDA (\code{backend =
"cuda"}, or \code{"default"} with \code{XHDFE_GPU_BACKEND=cuda}):
\code{TRUE} requests it, \code{FALSE} does not, \code{NULL} (default) the
process default, which is the environment variable
\code{XHDFE_REDUCED_PCG_CUDA} (read once per process; \code{1} requests
it) and otherwise off. A \code{"reghdfe-comparable"} fit that meets the
tier's other conditions and uses no mobility profile, absorption cache or
FE-structure cache then attempts the tier with the products of the
reduced system computed on the GPU; the recurrence, the stopping rule,
the acceptance trigger and the certificate are the tier's, computed on
the CPU. A published fit reports \code{absorption_method_code} 8,
\code{reduced_pcg_status} 1, \code{gpu_used} 1 and \code{gpu_status}
\code{"used"}; requesting CUDA alone does not prove entry, so check all of
them. A declined attempt runs the CUDA route that the fit takes without
the request. With the request, a fit kept out by a mobility profile or a
cache reports \code{reduced_pcg_reason} \code{"mobility_profile"},
\code{"absorption_cache"} or \code{"fe_structure_cache"} (the first that
applies, in that order) instead of \code{"gpu"} and runs that same route.
A file \code{xhdfe_mobility_profile.txt} in the working directory is a
mobility profile. An attempt that cannot obtain device memory, or cannot
page-lock the rows it exchanges with the GPU, declines with
\code{reduced_pcg_reason} \code{"memory"} or \code{"pinned"} and runs that
same route. Where that confirmed stopping rule is in force (a requested
tolerance of at least \code{1.25e-12} and
\code{XHDFE_REDUCED_PCG_CHAT_STOP} not \code{0}), the product used to
recompute the reduced residual is computed on the GPU as well; the
stopping decisions stay on the CPU.
\code{XHDFE_REDUCED_PCG_CUDA_REPLACEMENT=0}, set in the environment
before the first fit (read once per process), keeps that product on the
CPU. The acceptance trigger and the certificate, computed on the CPU, are
unchanged. \code{reduced_pcg = FALSE} keeps
both the tier and its CUDA variant off; on the CPU the argument has no
effect.}

\item{reduced_pcg_apply_fine}{the finer blocks of the segment pass of the
reduced-system tier's operator for this fit, where the tier's kernel for
that pass runs (builds compiled with GCC; an absorbed effect whose rows of
the reduced system take at least 2 MiB): an integer from 1 to 8 asks for
that factor, which is then halved while the private sums of the small
effects would take more than 4 MiB and kept only if its extra bytes fit the
attempt's memory budget; \code{1} keeps the blocks of 2.34.2;
\code{"auto"} starts from 8 and halves the factor while the cells of the
operator's segments per finer block stay below 16 384, then applies the
same rules (a rule of the data only, never of the threads); \code{NULL}
(default) the process value, the environment variable
\code{XHDFE_REDUCED_PCG_APPLY_FINE} (read once per process; \code{auto} or
an integer from 0 to 8, 0 read as 1) and otherwise the built-in value,
\code{1}. A factor above 1 changes the last bits of the fits the kernel
takes, within the precision contract. When an attempt that took finer
blocks declines because an allocation failed, the tier repeats it once on
the coarse blocks and publishes that second result, and the fit writes no
absorption-cache entry. The \code{reduced_pcg_fine_*} result fields report
what happened.}

\item{slope_solver}{optional reduced solver for eligible unweighted OLS
with two ordinary fixed effects and two slope dimensions on the same
identifiers, at least one with an intercept. \code{"schur"} selects the
diagonal preconditioner (CPU and CUDA); \code{"network"} selects the
forest preconditioner and \code{"direct"} the sparse direct solve (CPU).
Enable the reduced-system tier and use automatic absorption and convergence
in Comparable mode without saved effects or a saved group variable. Turn off
the absorption cache, FE-structure cache and mobility profile for these fits.
CUDA also requires \code{reduced_pcg_cuda = TRUE} or its environment default.
\code{"off"} disables the solver and both
helpers. \code{NULL} follows the per-fit environment switches
\code{XHDFE_EXPERIMENT_SLOPE_DIRECT},
\code{XHDFE_EXPERIMENT_SLOPE_NETWORK}, \code{XHDFE_EXPERIMENT_SLOPE_SCHUR}
in that order, each with value \code{1}, otherwise off.}

\item{slope_scores}{optional logical for eligible two-way cluster score
reuse, when the partitions match the ordinary effects. \code{NULL} enables
it with an explicit solver mode, or follows
\code{XHDFE_EXPERIMENT_NETWORK_SCORES=1} for an environment solver request.}

\item{slope_csr_owner}{optional logical for parallel sparse assembly.
\code{NULL} enables it with an explicit solver mode, or follows
\code{XHDFE_EXPERIMENT_NETWORK_CSR_OWNER=1} for an environment solver request.}
}
\section{Fail-closed input and output contracts}{
Estimation inputs are validated before any work is done. Non-finite
values (\code{NaN}, \code{Inf}) in the outcome, the regressors, the
instruments or the slope variables raise an error identifying the
offending row (and column), as do negative fixed-effect, cluster,
worker, firm, group or individual identifiers. Note that identifier
codings which use a negative value for "missing" -- Python's
\code{pandas.factorize()} returns \code{-1} -- are therefore rejected
rather than silently treated as a category or dropped.

On the output side, a fit reported as converged never carries a
non-finite coefficient, nor a non-finite standard error for a
coefficient whose inference is identified; such a result is rejected
with an error. Inference is legitimately unavailable, and therefore
exempt, for omitted (collinear) terms, an unidentified intercept, a
saturated design, fewer than two clusters, and non-positive residual
degrees of freedom.

Do not infer that a regressor was dropped from the value of its standard
error: a dropped regressor has a zero coefficient whose standard error is
\code{NA} on most paths but zero on some degenerate ones. Read
\code{omitted_reason} instead (0 kept, 1 collinear with the absorbed fixed
effects, 2 other collinearity). Finite inputs can still overflow their FP64
cross-products when the design is scaled extremely (roughly
\code{abs(X) >= 1e153}). Such a fit raises an error rather than being
misclassified as ordinary collinearity; rescale the outcome and/or regressors.
}
\value{
An object of class \code{"xhdfe"}: a list whose fields are the R
analogue of Stata's stored results (the corresponding \code{e()} names
are given where applicable).

\strong{Coefficients and inference}
\describe{
  \item{\code{coefficients}}{named numeric vector of point estimates
    (\code{e(b)}). With absorbed fixed effects a reghdfe-compatible
    \code{"(Intercept)"} is appended after the regressors; IV
    coefficients follow the exogenous ones.}
  \item{\code{se}, \code{tvalues}, \code{pvalues}}{standard errors,
    t-statistics, and p-values aligned with \code{coefficients}
    (from \code{e(V)} and \code{e(df_r)}).}
  \item{\code{conf_int}}{two-column matrix of confidence bounds at
    \code{level} (columns labelled e.g. \code{"2.5 \%"} and
    \code{"97.5 \%"}).}
  \item{\code{vcov}}{variance-covariance matrix of the estimates
    (\code{e(V)}).}
  \item{\code{omitted}}{named integer codes per coefficient
    (\code{e(omit_reason)}): 0 kept, 1 collinear with the absorbed fixed
    effects, 2 other collinearity.}
  \item{\code{se_type}}{\code{"unadjusted"}, \code{"robust"}, or
    \code{"cluster"} (cf. \code{e(vce)}).}
  \item{\code{level}}{confidence level in percent.}
}

\strong{Sample and observation counts}
\describe{
  \item{\code{residuals}}{full-length residual vector (\code{n_input}
    entries) with \code{NA} on rows outside the estimation sample; in
    grouped fits the residuals are at the group level instead.}
  \item{\code{sample}}{integer indices of the input rows used in
    estimation, after \code{subset}, missing-value filtering, and
    singleton dropping -- the analogue of \code{e(sample)}. Empty in
    grouped fits.}
  \item{\code{nobs}}{number of observations used (\code{e(N)}).}
  \item{\code{nobs_full}}{observations before singleton dropping
    (\code{e(N_full)}).}
  \item{\code{num_singletons}}{singleton observations dropped
    (\code{e(num_singletons)}).}
  \item{\code{nobs_effective}, \code{nobs_full_effective},
    \code{num_singletons_effective}}{frequency-weight effective counts:
    with \code{weights_type = "frequency"} these are sums of weights
    (replicated-rows semantics); otherwise they equal the row counts.}
  \item{\code{sumweights}}{sum of weights, or \code{nobs} if unweighted
    (\code{e(sumweights)}).}
  \item{\code{n_input}}{number of rows of the input data before any
    filtering.}
  \item{\code{grouped_fit}}{\code{TRUE} for \code{group}/
    \code{individual} collapsed fits.}
}

\strong{Degrees of freedom}
\describe{
  \item{\code{df_r}}{residual degrees of freedom after small-sample
    corrections, used for t-statistics, p-values, and confidence
    intervals (\code{e(df_r)}).}
  \item{\code{df_r_unadj}}{unadjusted residual degrees of freedom,
    N - k - df_a (\code{e(df_r_unadj)}).}
  \item{\code{df_m}}{model degrees of freedom (\code{e(df_m)}).}
  \item{\code{rank}}{effective model rank, omitted coefficients excluded
    (\code{e(rank)}).}
  \item{\code{df_a}}{absorbed degrees of freedom under the selected
    convention (\code{e(df_a)}); inspect \code{fe_inexact} for approximate counts.}
  \item{\code{df_a_levels}}{absorbed levels (\code{e(df_a_levels)}).}
  \item{\code{df_a_exact}}{absorbed levels adjusted for collinearity
    (\code{e(df_a_exact)}).}
  \item{\code{df_a_nested}}{absorbed levels nested within clusters, if
    any (\code{e(df_a_nested)}).}
  \item{\code{df_a_initial}}{absorbed levels before redundancy
    adjustments (\code{e(df_a_initial)}).}
  \item{\code{df_a_redundant}}{redundant absorbed degrees of freedom
    (\code{e(df_a_redundant)}).}
}

\strong{Fit statistics}
\describe{
  \item{\code{r2}, \code{r2_within}}{overall and within R-squared
    (\code{e(r2)}, \code{e(r2_within)}). Both are \code{NA} when the
    corresponding total sum of squares is zero (a constant outcome),
    matching \code{regress} and \code{reghdfe}.}
  \item{\code{r2_a}, \code{r2_a_within}}{adjusted (within) R-squared,
    computed with the unadjusted residual degrees of freedom, as in
    \code{reghdfe} (\code{e(r2_a)}, \code{e(r2_a_within)}); forced to 1
    in saturated fits.}
  \item{\code{rss}, \code{tss}, \code{tss_within}, \code{mss}}{residual,
    total, within-total, and model (tss - rss) sums of squares
    (\code{e(rss)}, \code{e(tss)}, \code{e(tss_within)}, \code{e(mss)}).
    When the fixed-effect part contains only slope terms
    (\code{fe[[x]]}, no level dimension), the model has no intercept and
    \code{tss}/\code{r2} use the \emph{uncentered} total sum of squares,
    matching \code{reghdfe} and \code{regress, noconstant}.}
  \item{\code{sigma2}}{estimated residual variance (\code{e(sigma2)}).}
  \item{\code{rmse}}{root mean squared error,
    sqrt(rss / df_r_unadj-style denominator), matching \code{reghdfe}
    (\code{e(rmse)}).}
  \item{\code{F_stat}, \code{F_p}}{model F-statistic and p-value
    (\code{e(F)}, \code{e(p)}): a Wald test that all kept non-intercept
    coefficients are zero, using a generalized inverse of their
    covariance block.}
  \item{\code{ll}, \code{ll_0}}{log-likelihood and log-likelihood at
    zeroed regressors, within (\code{e(ll)}, \code{e(ll_0)}).}
  \item{\code{saturated}}{1 if the fitted design is saturated /
    perfect-fit, 0 otherwise (\code{e(saturated)}).}
  \item{\code{has_intercept}}{whether a constant is reported
    (\code{e(report_constant)}).}
}

\strong{Absorbed-fixed-effect diagnostics} (one entry per absorbed
dimension, mirroring \code{reghdfe}'s degrees-of-freedom footnote table,
\code{e(dof_table)})
\describe{
  \item{\code{fe_num_levels}}{number of categories after singleton
    dropping.}
  \item{\code{fe_base_levels}}{raw number of levels before adjustments.}
  \item{\code{fe_redundant}}{redundant coefficients detected in the
    dimension.}
  \item{\code{fe_num_coefs}}{number of coefficients contributed by the
    dimension.}
  \item{\code{fe_inexact}}{flag: the redundancy count is an inexact
    (pairwise) estimate.}
  \item{\code{fe_nested}}{flag: the dimension is nested within a cluster
    variable.}
  \item{\code{fe_labels}}{labels of the absorbed dimensions (deparsed
    fixed-effect terms; cf. \code{e(absorb_labels)}).}
}

\strong{Solver and backend diagnostics}
\describe{
  \item{\code{iterations}}{absorber iterations (\code{e(iterations)}).}
  \item{\code{converged}}{1 if converged, 0 otherwise
    (\code{e(converged)}).}
  \item{\code{abs_residual}, \code{abs_residual_rel}}{explicit absolute and
    relative normal-equation residuals verified after absorption.}
  \item{\code{krylov_internal_tolerance},
    \code{krylov_max_final_backward_error},
    \code{krylov_max_condition}, and
    \code{krylov_max_condition_times_backward_error}}{ordinary no-slope
    LSMR/MLSMR solve tolerance and deterministic maxima across right-hand
    sides; \code{krylov_max_condition} is the maximum running condition
    estimate. These fields are zero when that Krylov path was not used.}
  \item{Krylov-PCG scope}{The separate PCG path is not covered by this
    parity floor and reports zeros; a PCG floor remains future reviewed work.
    The reduced-system tier is a different conjugate-gradient solver with
    the stopping rule described under \code{absorption_method}; it leaves
    these four fields at zero itself.}
  \item{\code{precision_certified}}{whether the verified relative residual
    meets the numerical certificate limit. In the combined
    \code{group}/\code{individual} path this check is authoritative:
    additional certification sweeps are included in \code{iterations}, and
    \code{converged} is true only when this field is also true.}
  \item{\code{absorption_method_used}}{name of the method effectively
    used (\code{"auto"}, \code{"gauss-seidel"},
    \code{"symmetric-gauss-seidel"}, \code{"jacobi"}, \code{"schwarz"},
    \code{"lsmr"}, \code{"mlsmr"}, \code{"auto-mlsmr"},
    \code{"reduced-pcg"} when the reduced-system tier published the fit, or
    \code{"krylov-pcg"} when the forced Krylov-PCG route did).}
  \item{\code{absorption_method_code}}{numeric code with the same table
    as Stata's \code{e(absorption_method_used)}: 0 auto, 1 gauss-seidel,
    2 symmetric-gauss-seidel, 3 jacobi, 4 schwarz, 5 lsmr, 6 mlsmr,
    7 auto-mlsmr, 8 reduced-pcg, 9 krylov-pcg.}
  \item{\code{reduced_pcg_status}, \code{reduced_pcg_reason},
    \code{reduced_pcg_discarded_iterations}}{the reduced-system tier (see
    \code{absorption_method}): status 0 not applicable (off, ineligible,
    not reached, or another route's transform read from the absorption
    cache), 1 the tier published the fit (fresh or read back from the
    cache), 2 the attempt declined and the route ran from the original
    data; the reason (\code{"none"} when published, otherwise the
    ineligibility or decline reason, such as \code{"weights"} or
    \code{"stall"}, or \code{"off"}, \code{"not_reached"} or
    \code{"cache"}); and the iterations of a declined attempt, 0 otherwise.
    With status 1, \code{iterations} counts the tier's iterations (plus
    those of a normal-equation correction, should one follow)
    (\code{e(reduced_pcg_status)}, \code{e(reduced_pcg_reason)},
    \code{e(reduced_pcg_discarded_iterations)}).}
  \item{\code{reduced_pcg_fine_request}, \code{reduced_pcg_fine_source},
    \code{reduced_pcg_fine_used}, \code{reduced_pcg_fine_parts},
    \code{reduced_pcg_hinted_applies}, \code{reduced_pcg_fine_alloc_failed},
    \code{reduced_pcg_fine_retry}, \code{reduced_pcg_fine_retry_factor},
    \code{reduced_pcg_fine_retry_parts},
    \code{reduced_pcg_fine_retry_iters},
    \code{reduced_pcg_cache_hit}}{the finer blocks of the tier's segment
    pass (see \code{reduced_pcg_apply_fine}): the request in force
    (\code{"auto"} or \code{"1"} to \code{"8"}) and its source
    (\code{"request"}, \code{"environment"} or \code{"built-in"}); the
    factor the attempt chose (1 the coarse blocks; 0 when no attempt on the
    CPU reached that choice) and its blocks; the applications of the
    operator through the kernel; whether the finer blocks could not be
    allocated; whether the attempt was repeated on the coarse blocks after a
    memory decline, with the factor, blocks and iterations of the discarded
    first attempt; and whether the absorption came from the absorption cache
    (\code{e(reduced_pcg_fine_request)} and the other \code{e()} results of
    the same names). Read the factor and the applications together: the
    factor and its blocks say which blocks the attempt chose, not that the
    kernel ran on them; \code{reduced_pcg_hinted_applies} is 0 when the chosen
    blocks were applied in none.}
  \item{\code{slope_solver_request}, \code{slope_solver_source},
    \code{slope_solver_status}, \code{slope_solver_reason},
    \code{slope_solver_used}}{effective request, origin, outcome, reason and
    mode of the published slope solver (\code{"none"} when unused). The
    status is \code{"off"}, \code{"ineligible"}, \code{"declined"} or
    \code{"accepted"}.}
  \item{\code{slope_solver_discarded_iters}, \code{slope_solver_scores_used},
    \code{slope_solver_csr_owner_used}}{discarded iterations, whether the
    published inference used the scores, and whether the owner assembly
    ran successfully (including before a subsequent solver decline).}
  \item{\code{threads_requested}, \code{threads_effective}}{raw request
    (0 means auto) and the request after runtime/automatic limits.}
  \item{\code{threads_used}}{largest OpenMP team observed executing a real
    xhdfe loop.}
  \item{\code{parallel_workers_active}}{largest number of distinct workers
    processing useful work in one observed region.}
  \item{\code{thread_capacity}, \code{openmp_enabled},
    \code{thread_limit_code}, \code{thread_limit_reason}}{runtime-visible
    capacity, build capability, and any applied limit.}
  \item{\code{tolerance_mode}}{tolerance mode used
    (\code{e(tolerance_mode)}).}
  \item{\code{gpu_used}}{1 if the GPU backend was effectively used, 0 if
    the CPU path was used (\code{e(gpu_used)}).}
  \item{\code{gpu_status_code}, \code{gpu_status}}{status code 0-5 or 7 and
    its label (\code{e(gpu_status_code)}, \code{e(gpu_status)}); see the
    \emph{GPU backends} section for the taxonomy.}
  \item{\code{gpu_attempted}}{1 if GPU absorption was attempted
    (\code{e(gpu_attempted)}).}
  \item{\code{gpu_absorption_converged},
    \code{gpu_absorption_iterations}}{convergence flag and iteration
    count of the attempted GPU absorption; missing if not attempted
    (\code{e(gpu_absorption_converged)},
    \code{e(gpu_absorption_iterations)}).}
  \item{\code{cuda_tail_code}, \code{cuda_tail}}{how the CUDA path
    completed the projection of an unweighted Comparable fit with three
    fixed effects: 0 \code{"none"} (no completion ran, or the restart
    after a conjugate-gradient stage stayed on the GPU, as in 2.30.1), 1
    \code{"device-lsmr"} (on the GPU), 2 \code{"host-mlsmr"} (on the CPU
    with MLSMR, after the GPU sweeps, or from the original data when a
    conjugate-gradient stage stopped without converging; \code{gpu_used}
    stays 1; \code{iterations} then counts every GPU and CPU iteration
    within one \code{maxiter} budget; a conjugate-gradient stage that
    converges counts only its own iterations, on the CPU and the GPU)
    (\code{e(cuda_tail_code)}, \code{e(cuda_tail)}).}
  \item{\code{backend_requested}}{the \code{backend} argument value
    (cf. \code{e(gpu_backend_requested)}).}
}

\strong{Fixed-effect recovery} (populated with \code{save_fe = TRUE})
\describe{
  \item{\code{fe_effects}}{named list with one numeric vector of
    per-observation fixed-effect contributions per absorbed dimension,
    on the estimation sample; use \code{fixef()} for full-length,
    NA-padded vectors.}
  \item{\code{fe_recovery_converged}, \code{fe_recovery_iterations},
    \code{fe_recovery_max_delta}}{recovery convergence flag, iteration
    count, and maximum change in the final recovery sweep
    (\code{e(fe_recovery_converged)}, \code{e(fe_recovery_iterations)},
    \code{e(fe_recovery_max_delta)}).}
  \item{\code{groupvar}}{with \code{groupvar = TRUE}: 1-based ids of the
    first mobility group (connected component), the analogue of Stata's
    \code{groupvar(newvar)}; otherwise \code{NULL}.}
}

\strong{Cluster diagnostics} (populated when clustered)
\describe{
  \item{\code{num_clusters}}{minimum number of clusters across
    dimensions (\code{e(N_clust)}).}
  \item{\code{cluster_counts}}{cluster counts by dimension
    (\code{e(N_clust#)}).}
  \item{\code{cluster_combo_counts}}{counts for each cluster combination
    in bitmask order (\code{e(cluster_diag)}).}
  \item{\code{cluster_scale}}{cluster scaling factor from the
    small-sample corrections (\code{e(cluster_scale)}).}
  \item{\code{cluster_names}}{names of the cluster dimensions
    (\code{e(clustvar)}).}
  \item{\code{vcv_psd_fixed}}{1 if the multiway-cluster variance matrix
    required the Cameron-Gelbach-Miller positive-semi-definite
    adjustment (\code{e(vcv_psd_fixed)}); a warning is printed, matching
    \code{reghdfe}.}
}

\strong{Prediction caches and bookkeeping}
\describe{
  \item{\code{xb_cache}, \code{y_cache}, \code{stdp_cache}}{full-length
    caches (NA off-sample) of the linear prediction, the outcome, and
    the standard error of the linear prediction; they back
    \code{predict()} and \code{fitted()} and are absent in grouped
    fits.}
  \item{\code{call}}{the matched call.}
  \item{\code{version}}{the package version string (\code{e(version)}).}
}

\strong{Formula-interface extras} (set by \code{xhdfe()}, not by
\code{xhdfe_fit()})
\describe{
  \item{\code{fml}}{the model formula.}
  \item{\code{terms}}{the \code{terms} object of the regressor part
    (used by \code{predict(..., newdata = )}).}
  \item{\code{terms_endo}}{the \code{terms} object of the endogenous
    part for IV fits (\code{NULL} otherwise); \code{predict} uses it to
    rebuild the endogenous columns on new data.}
  \item{\code{xlevels}, \code{contrasts}}{factor levels and contrasts
    recorded at fit time so \code{predict(..., newdata = )} expands
    factors exactly as during estimation.}
  \item{\code{data_name}}{deparsed name of the \code{data} argument.}
  \item{\code{weights_type}}{\code{"analytic"}, \code{"frequency"},
    \code{"importance"}, or \code{"probability"}
    when weights were supplied, otherwise \code{NULL}.}
  \item{\code{group_name}, \code{individual_name},
    \code{aggregation}}{deparsed group/individual arguments and the
    aggregation method when group mode is used (\code{e(group)},
    \code{e(individual)}, \code{e(aggregation)}).}
}
}
\description{
\code{xhdfe()} estimates linear models with any number of high-dimensional
fixed effects through the xhdfe C++ backend -- the same compiled estimator
behind the Stata \code{xhdfe} command and the Python \code{xhdfe} package.
It mirrors \code{reghdfe}'s reporting conventions and defaults where
possible (singleton dropping, degrees-of-freedom accounting, multiway
clustered inference, constant reporting) while also exposing
\pkg{fixest}-style small-sample corrections.

The Stata command's six syntax forms translate to R as follows:
\itemize{
  \item Least-squares regression without fixed effects:
    \code{xhdfe(y ~ x1 + x2, data = df)}.
  \item Regression with absorbed fixed effects (Stata
    \code{absorb(fe1 fe2)}): \code{xhdfe(y ~ x1 + x2 | fe1 + fe2, data = df)}.
  \item Group-level outcomes (Stata \code{group(gid)}):
    \code{xhdfe(y ~ x, data = df, group = ~ gid)}, optionally with a
    fixed-effects part.
  \item Group-level outcomes with individual fixed effects (Stata
    \code{group(gid) individual(iid)}):
    \code{xhdfe(y ~ x | iid, data = df, group = ~ gid, individual = ~ iid)}.
  \item Instrumental variables / 2SLS (Stata \code{endogenous()} +
    \code{instruments()}):
    \code{xhdfe(y ~ exo | fe1 + fe2 | endo ~ inst, data = df)}; without
    absorbed fixed effects, \code{xhdfe(y ~ exo | endo ~ inst, data = df)}.
  \item Version information (Stata \code{xhdfe, version}):
    \code{xhdfe_info()}.
}

With absorbed fixed effects a \code{reghdfe}-compatible constant
\code{"(Intercept)"} is reported after the regressors; remove it with
\code{0 +} in the regressor part (the analogue of Stata's
\code{noconstant}).

Standard S3 methods are available on the returned object:
\code{print}, \code{summary}, \code{coef}, \code{vcov}, \code{confint},
\code{nobs}, \code{df.residual}, \code{residuals}, \code{fitted},
\code{predict} (with Stata's \code{xb}, \code{xbd}, \code{d},
\code{residuals}, \code{dresiduals}, \code{score}, and \code{stdp}
statistics), \code{logLik}, \code{deviance}, \code{formula},
\code{fixef}, and \code{coeftable}. Unlike Stata, no
\code{residuals()}-style option is needed before using the fixed-effect
prediction types: residuals and prediction caches are always retained
(except in grouped fits).
}
\section{Absorbing fixed effects}{

The second formula part lists the fixed effects to absorb; each term is
equivalent to including one dummy per category, without ever building the
dummies. The grammar (fixest-compatible) and its Stata
\code{absorb()} equivalents:

\tabular{lll}{
  \strong{R term} \tab \strong{Stata absorb()} \tab \strong{Meaning} \cr
  \code{fe} \tab \code{fe} \tab absorbed intercepts (one per level) \cr
  \code{f1^f2} \tab \code{f1#f2} \tab combined (interaction) fixed
    effect \cr
  \code{fe[x]} \tab \code{fe##c.x} \tab absorbed intercepts plus
    group-specific slopes on \code{x} \cr
  \code{fe[[x]]} \tab \code{fe#c.x} \tab group-specific slopes only \cr
  \code{fe[x1, x2]} \tab \code{fe##c.x1 fe#c.x2} \tab several slopes on
    one carrier \cr
}

Notes:
\itemize{
  \item \emph{Categorical interactions.} \code{f1^f2} builds the joint
    fixed effect of the two id variables. Like \code{reghdfe} -- which
    canonicalizes a categorical-only \code{f1##f2} to the same joint
    fixed effect as \code{f1#f2} -- it is \emph{not} expanded into main
    effects plus interaction; write \code{f1 + f2 + f1^f2} explicitly if
    all three dimensions are intended.
  \item \emph{Heterogeneous slopes.} Slope variables must be numeric and
    of full data length. The core absorbs at most one slope per carrier
    dimension; multiple slopes on the same ids (\code{fe[x1, x2]}) are
    handled internally by duplicating the carrier dimension -- one
    absorbed dimension per slope variable, with the intercept (if
    requested) riding on the first -- exactly like Stata expands
    \code{absorb(fe##c.x1 fe#c.x2)}.
  \item \emph{Optional reduced slope solver.}
    \code{slope_solver = "schur"} requests the reduced equation solver for
    eligible fits. See \code{slope_solver} for mode precedence and the
    two assembly and inference controls. Accepted fits report method 9 and
    pass the slope certificate; the diagnostics also describe a declined
    attempt or an ineligible request.
  \item \emph{Id encoding.} Integer id vectors are passed to the core
    verbatim (level handling is the core's job); factors use their level
    codes; character, logical, and non-integral numeric vectors are
    encoded by first appearance. The same rules apply to cluster,
    group, and individual ids.
  \item \emph{Restrictions.} Heterogeneous slopes are not supported with
    \code{group}/\code{individual} or with the LSMR-family absorption
    methods (\code{"lsmr"}, \code{"mlsmr"}, \code{"auto-mlsmr"}).
}
}

\section{Tolerance modes}{

The nominal tolerance \code{tol} only has a precise meaning together with
\code{tolerance_mode}. Under the default \code{"reghdfe-comparable"} mode
(the default since 2.7.0) the absorber stops when one full sweep moves
the working data by less than \code{tol} in relative norm -- the same
meaning \code{reghdfe} attaches to its tolerance -- so coefficients match
\code{reghdfe} at the same nominal tolerance, down to problem
conditioning. \code{"xhdfe-fast"} restores the pre-2.7.0 stopping rule:
typically about 1.5-3x fewer absorber iterations, but its effective
precision is data-dependent and can be looser than the nominal tolerance
on ill-conditioned (e.g. sparse bipartite) designs; it is appropriate for
exploration and speed benchmarking, and any published timing should state
the mode used. Combined group/individual CUDA fits nevertheless use an
internal \code{min(tol, 1e-12)} floor before certification, so fast mode
cannot re-enable a materially inaccurate early GS stop.
\code{"strict-residual"} is a heavier audit mode that
treats a final absolute check as authoritative, may use extra iterations
up to \code{maxiter}, and reports non-convergence when the check is not
met; it is not supported with heterogeneous slopes. On CUDA with the
automatic method and criterion, without weights, \code{save_fe} or
\code{group}, the check is the largest absolute group mean of the
delivered residuals in the units of the dependent variable (see
\code{tolerance_mode}).

New in 2.11.0: under the default mode, absorption on ill-conditioned,
poorly connected multiway fixed-effect graphs is transparently handed off
to a stable per-column conjugate-gradient solver on the symmetric
demeaning operator, on CPU and CUDA alike. Results on well-conditioned
problems are numerically identical to prior versions; on pathological
graphs the handoff can cut runtime by an order of magnitude at equal or
better precision.

The mode used is recorded in the \code{tolerance_mode} field of the
result (Stata \code{e(tolerance_mode)}).
}

\section{GPU backends}{

\code{backend = "cuda"} requests the CUDA absorber. It requires a package
build with CUDA support, e.g.
\preformatted{XHDFE_ENABLE_CUDA=ON XHDFE_CUDA_ARCH=90 R CMD INSTALL xhdfe}
where \code{XHDFE_CUDA_ARCH} selects a single target compute capability
(default 90, e.g. an NVIDIA H100; the backend requires compute capability
7.5 or newer and the NVCC compiler). The equivalent Stata plugin build is
\code{XHDFE_ENABLE_CUDA=ON XHDFE_CUDA_ARCH=90 bash
stata/tools/build-plugin.sh --linux --openmp}.

\strong{Fail-closed contract.} Requesting \code{"cuda"} (or
\code{"metal"}) never silently falls back to the CPU: if the backend is
unavailable, fails, does not converge on the GPU, or a CPU cache/profile
result would be returned, the fit stops with an error -- mirroring the
Stata command, which exits with error 498 in the same situations. The one
exception is reported: see \strong{Weighted fits on CUDA} below.
After a successful GPU fit, validate real GPU use through the
diagnostics on the result object: \code{gpu_used} must be \code{1} and
\code{gpu_status} must be \code{"used"} (Stata: \code{e(gpu_used)==1},
\code{e(gpu_backend)=="cuda"}, \code{e(gpu_status)=="used"}). The status
taxonomy (\code{gpu_status_code} / \code{gpu_status}) is:

\strong{Certification boundary.} A GPU-use diagnostic proves execution,
not universal numerical equivalence. Validation identifies the tested data,
specifications and exact artifacts. A failed convergence or precision check
raises an informative error and returns no estimates. Explicit CUDA requests
do not substitute CPU estimates for a device result, except the reported
weighted CPU route below (\code{gpu_status_code} 7), and Metal requests are rejected until that
backend is implemented. Poor conditioning can amplify a small residual;
no fixed tolerance guarantees arbitrary coefficient accuracy on every model.

Small group/individual designs (at most 1500 FE columns and two million
cells after merging identical incidence rows) are projected by a dense
column-pivoted QR with iterative refinement, exact to rounding, in every
tolerance mode; directions whose relative pivot is below 1e-10 are
either exact redundancies (dropped) or, when the exact rational rank of
the incidence design exceeds the numerical rank, FP64-ambiguous: such a
fit is refused only when the fitted residual depends on the unresolved
direction (FE-space forward certificate), never for data that do not.
Larger designs use the Krylov solver, whose dual stopping test is accepted
only when the running condition estimate times the dual residual meets
the public forward target (100 times the mode's dual tolerance, at least
1e-8); in reghdfe-comparable and strict-residual modes a residual
refinement then removes nearly null incidence directions, applied to all
right-hand sides or to none and only after the primary solution passes
the per-column certificate, so 2.26.2 results are the floor and xhdfe-fast
keeps its 2.26.2 stopping rule. The shared OLS stage uses
additional arithmetic precision only when the FP64 rank decision is not
separated from its threshold. Large level offsets alone do not justify
omitting a regressor with within variation: the collinearity rule compares
the within variation with the centered scale of the column whenever the
model contains a constant. Heavy sufficient certificates (FE-space forward
bound, exact redundancy proofs, per-row N1 evidence) run only under
\code{XHDFE_CERTIFY=1}.

Version 2.30.1 corrects an inherited CUDA Comparable covariance error for
ordinary models with three fixed effects, no weights and no saved effects.
The remaining projection is completed at the existing target within the
total iteration budget. GPU repair counters include all attempted work;
old absorption caches are invalidated automatically. The release record
documents the accepted runtime cost relative to 2.29.1. Fast retains its
separate approximation contract.

\strong{Weighted fits on CUDA.} With \code{weighted_cpu_route = TRUE},
a fit with weights (any type, all ones
and zero weights included) and two or more absorbed effect dimensions in
\code{reghdfe-comparable} or \code{strict-residual}, whose effective
backend is CUDA (\code{backend = "cuda"}, \code{XHDFE_GPU_BACKEND=cuda},
or a build whose default backend is CUDA), is absorbed on the CPU and says
so: \code{gpu_used} 0 and \code{gpu_status_code} 7
(\code{"weighted_cpu_route"}). \code{xhdfe_demean()} keeps its CUDA
partial-out route with weights. \code{backend_requested} stays \code{"default"} for a request made by the
environment. On CUDA such fits could pass the certificate and still lie
outside the comparable contract against reghdfe at tolerance 1e-12
(coefficients up to 4.5e-7 and standard errors up to 2.8e-5 relative on a
public fixture). The dimensions are counted as the fit receives them,
before redundant ones are removed. A weighted fit with \code{group} and
without \code{individual} is the collapsed weighted fit and is routed too;
its CPU fit runs, as before, without the routing retry and without the
saved-effects projection (the \code{group} entry turns both off). With
\code{individual} a fit keeps the GPU route and was not measured against
the contract on CUDA; \code{xhdfe-fast}, unweighted fits and fits with one
absorbed effect keep the GPU route. A routed fit takes the time of the CPU
backend. Its results other than the GPU status code are bit for bit those
of the same fit with \code{backend = "cpu"}, with the same package build,
threads and initial cache and profile state, wherever that CPU fit repeats
itself (an automatic fit that benchmarks methods by time while writing a
mobility profile from empty does not); the identity is stated for builds
whose default backend is the CPU (all released ones). In the ordinary
weighted Auto CPU path, the published Comparable projection may use
the ordinary selector with four effects and \code{save_fe}, or for
just-identified IV with three effects. For ordinary weighted CPU fits with
three fixed effects and without saved effects, explicit
\code{absorption_method = "gauss-seidel"} in Comparable mode checks each
column's fixed-effect moments and can perform one Gauss--Seidel block
correction within \code{maxiter}. A routed \code{strict-residual} fit
is the CPU's strict fit. An explicit \code{absorption_method = "schwarz"}
with weights gets the CPU's answer, which for an ordinary fit is a refusal.
A routed fit is the CPU fit also for the optional absorption cache and
mobility profile, so in a session that writes profiles a later fit may read
what a routed fit wrote, as it reads what a CPU fit wrote.
\code{weighted_cpu_route = FALSE} selects the ordinary device route for
a fit that requests CUDA.

\strong{Known limitations.}
With \code{retain_fes = TRUE} in Comparable mode, a fit can publish a
separate certified within projection and recover the effects afterwards.
Its outcome reconstruction is then limited by FE recovery accuracy.
Explicit methods, IV, heterogeneous slopes and grouped fits can publish
the effect-capturing sweep, whose accuracy is design-dependent.
Ordinary CUDA fits with two to four weighted
categorical effects or four unweighted effects check the fixed-effect moments.
A measured failure can trigger one device LSMR projection within the budget.
For analytic, probability and importance weights, the Comparable
correction covers up to 2,000,000 estimation rows.
On CUDA, residuals have the accuracy of the CUDA fit. On Windows,
near-perfect additive-cell fixtures returned exact coefficients and
certified fits with relative covariance errors of 2.65e-9, 1.74e-4 and
1.82e+2 at relative residual noise of 2^-12, 2^-20 and 2^-30 (2.2e-16 on
Linux): the exact additive-cell projection needs extended precision, which
Windows hosts run at 53 bits.
A fit that converges by the solver's rule but
does not pass the precision certificate returns no estimates in R, Python
and Stata alike. Ill-conditioned specifications can still require a refusal.
In \code{reghdfe-comparable}, heterogeneous slopes under an explicit
\code{convergence = "reghdfe"} can require continuation at tighter internal
tolerances, returning the first certified stage. The default \code{"auto"}
checks the slope block; \code{xhdfe-fast} keeps its aggregate certificate
without the Comparable block continuation. \code{aggregation = "sum"} without an ordinary
absorbed fixed effect spans the constant only for a uniform team size or
through a data-dependent combination of individual columns, decided by
projecting the ones vector on the absorbed effects as one separate
right-hand side; when it is not spanned
the fit has no intercept and is reported as one (\code{model_has_constant}
is \code{FALSE}, no \code{(Intercept)} entry, uncentered total sum of
squares), with coefficients, RSS and residuals unchanged. CUDA with small, ill-conditioned group/individual
designs can leave residuals at the 1e-6 relative level where the CPU
direct projection is exact; on designs small enough for that projection the
device result is compared with it and refused with an informative error when
a retained column deviates by more than ten times the forward tolerance of
the mode; use the CPU backend for such designs. CUDA with
\code{tolerance_mode = "reghdfe-comparable"} on ill-conditioned ordinary
designs also checks original-regressor normal equations for unweighted OLS
without saved effects. A failed check receives one continuation with the same
method and tolerance, within the remaining iteration budget. A persistent
failure returns no estimates and does not substitute CPU estimates. This
check admits the directors and difficult 10M-row 3-FE benchmark fits covered
by the 2.29.0 CUDA validation, with bounded corrections. Other ill-conditioned
specifications can still require a refusal. The opt-in accuracy-retry settings
cannot bypass this necessary check. The
individual block subtracts one exact redundancy against an ordinary fixed
effect whenever the constant is provably in the span of the membership
columns (mean aggregation, or sum with a uniform team size);
\code{dofadjustments = "exact"} computes the exact rank.

\tabular{lll}{
  \strong{Code} \tab \strong{Label} \tab \strong{Meaning} \cr
  0 \tab \code{not_requested} \tab GPU not requested \cr
  1 \tab \code{used} \tab GPU backend effectively used \cr
  2 \tab \code{backend_unavailable} \tab requested backend unavailable \cr
  3 \tab \code{gpu_absorption_not_converged} \tab GPU absorption did not
    converge \cr
  4 \tab \code{gpu_backend_failed} \tab GPU backend failed \cr
  5 \tab \code{cpu_cache_or_profile_result} \tab a CPU cache/profile
    result was produced \cr
  7 \tab \code{weighted_cpu_route} \tab weighted fit with two or more
    absorbed effects run on the CPU by design \cr
}

As an alternative to the \code{backend} argument, the environment
variable \code{XHDFE_GPU_BACKEND} (\code{cpu}|\code{cuda}|\code{metal})
selects the backend; the \code{backend} argument temporarily sets that
variable around the fit and is the recommended interface.
\code{backend = "cpu"} forces the CPU path even when the environment
variable is set. \code{"metal"} is reserved for Metal builds and errors
where unavailable. CPU behavior is the reference implementation. The joint
group/individual LSMR route is CPU-only; CUDA \code{"auto"} retains the
existing GPU solver and now requires the authoritative certificate, while
explicit LSMR with CUDA is rejected.
}

\section{Group-level outcomes with individual fixed effects}{

\code{xhdfe()} supports individual fixed effects with group-level
outcomes (e.g. patent-level citations with inventor fixed effects). The
data must be in long format where the group and individual ids together
uniquely identify observations (the Stata help's
\code{isid patent_id inventor_id} requirement). The combining rules are:
\enumerate{
  \item no fixed effects, no \code{group}, no \code{individual}:
    ordinary least squares with a constant;
  \item only absorbed fixed effects: standard fixed-effects regression;
  \item \code{group} without \code{individual}: the same as (1)/(2) run
    on one observation per group (the data are collapsed to a
    representative row per group);
  \item \code{group} and \code{individual} (plus, optionally, absorbed
    fixed effects): a group-level fixed-effects regression in which each
    group's outcome loads on the \code{aggregation} (mean or sum) of its
    individuals' fixed-effect components.
}
The individual dimension is appended (last) to the absorbed dimensions
for stability if not already present, exactly as in Stata. In
grouped modes the core estimates on one representative row per group:
\code{sample} identifies those rows in the original input, residuals and
predictions are aligned to the input with \code{NA} outside that sample,
and \code{grouped_fit} is \code{TRUE}. \strong{Note:} singleton bookkeeping
happens \emph{after} the representative-row construction, so
\code{num_singletons} can differ from \code{reghdfe}'s pre-collapse row
count even when the effective estimation sample is identical.

On CPU, \code{absorption_method = "auto"} uses joint matrix-free LSMR
for the standard and individual dimensions; explicit \code{"lsmr"} selects
the same CPU route. The independent certificate remains mandatory after
any bounded refinement or tighter retry; the original final tolerance,
iteration budget and requested backend are preserved. Failure raises an
informative error without new estimates. CUDA \code{"auto"} keeps the existing
group/individual GPU solver and accepts it only after the authoritative
certificate passes. Explicit \code{"gauss-seidel"} and
\code{"symmetric-gauss-seidel"} remain supported; \code{"jacobi"},
\code{"schwarz"}, \code{"mlsmr"}, and \code{"auto-mlsmr"} are not.

IV/2SLS, heterogeneous slopes, and (with both \code{group} and
\code{individual}) \code{save_fe} are not supported in group mode.

Membership columns span a constant under \code{aggregation = "mean"}
and under \code{"sum"} with equal group sizes. Unequal-size sum
aggregation need not span a constant. If the intended model requires one,
include an ordinary FE, for example a variable \code{c} equal to one in
\code{y ~ x | c + inventor}. A reported intercept alone does not add that
missing direction to the grouped model. There is one residual per retained
group, on its selected input row; other group members have \code{NA}.
}

\section{Missing features and restrictions}{

\itemize{
  \item Heterogeneous slopes are not supported together with
    \code{group}/\code{individual}, nor with the LSMR-family absorption
    methods on standard FE designs.
  \item \code{save_fe} is not supported when both \code{group} and
    \code{individual} are specified.
  \item IV/2SLS is not supported in \code{group} mode.
  \item \code{tolerance_mode = "strict-residual"} is not supported with
    heterogeneous slopes (an error is raised before estimation).
  \item Explicit LSMR-family absorption requests are CPU-only and do not
    support \code{save_fe}; only plain LSMR is supported for combined
    \code{group}/\code{individual} fits.
  \item No HAC or Driscoll-Kraay standard errors (see \code{ivreghdfe}
    in the \code{reghdfe} ecosystem).
}

With IV/2SLS and absorbed fixed effects, the reported
\code{"(Intercept)"} is the normalization
\eqn{\bar y-\bar x^\prime\hat\beta}, with zero-mean absorbed
contributions. It is finite and internally consistent, but remains
normalization-dependent and must not be interpreted as a structural IV
parameter.

With absorbed fixed effects the \code{"(Intercept)"} is the constant
recovered by the FE normalisation, \eqn{\bar y-\bar x^\prime\hat\beta}.
When a one-way cluster nests an absorbed intercept dimension (every
cluster is a union of its levels; \code{fe[x]} keeps the levels,
\code{fe[[x]]} has none), the cluster sums of the residuals are exactly
zero and the intercept's row and column of the covariance matrix are
exactly \eqn{\bar x^\prime V\bar x} and \eqn{-V\bar x}, with \eqn{V} the
slope block; this applies on every absorption route and backend, but not
with \code{tolerance_mode = "xhdfe-fast"} or instruments. That variance is
zero when \eqn{\bar x = 0} or there are no regressors: the intercept's
standard error is then zero and its t statistic, p-value and confidence
interval are missing. This does not mean a structural parameter known
without uncertainty; the constant is a normalisation of the absorbed
effects. Saturated designs and fewer than two clusters keep their
missing-inference convention.
}

\section{Environment variables}{

The C++ core reads the following runtime knobs:
\describe{
  \item{\code{XHDFE_GPU_BACKEND}}{backend request
    (\code{cpu}|\code{cuda}|\code{metal}) when \code{backend = "default"}.
    An explicit \code{backend} argument overrides it for that fit through
    a scoped native selection.}
  \item{\code{XHDFE_AUTO_MLSMR_DEFAULT_MAX_FES}}{raises the default cap
    (3) on the number of fixed-effect dimensions eligible for the
    probe-based auto-MLSMR selector under
    \code{absorption_method = "auto"}.}
  \item{\code{XHDFE_MLSMR_AUTO_GATE}}{set to \code{0} to disable MLSMR
    promotions by the automatic gate entirely.}
  \item{\code{XHDFE_REDUCED_PCG}}{the process default of the
    reduced-system tier (see \code{absorption_method}): \code{0}, set before
    the first fit of the R session, turns it off for every fit that leaves
    \code{reduced_pcg = NULL}; it is read once per process. The argument
    \code{reduced_pcg} overrides it fit by fit. While the tier is on for a
    fit, absorption-cache entries written with it off or by an earlier
    version are not read, and while it is off, entries written with it on
    are not read.}
  \item{\code{XHDFE_REDUCED_PCG_CUDA}}{the process default of the
    experimental CUDA variant of the tier (see \code{reduced_pcg_cuda}):
    \code{1}, set before the first fit of the R session, requests it for
    every CUDA fit that leaves \code{reduced_pcg_cuda = NULL}; it is read
    once per process. The argument \code{reduced_pcg_cuda} overrides it fit
    by fit.}
  \item{\code{XHDFE_REDUCED_PCG_TRACE}}{\code{1} writes the tier's plan and
    decision to stderr; \code{2} adds the verified residual of each column
    of a published fit.}
  \item{\code{XHDFE_REDUCED_PCG_CHAT_STOP}}{\code{0}, set before the
    first fit of the R session, restores the tier's former
    stopping criterion. It is read once per process and
    changes neither the acceptance trigger nor the certificate. If the
    confirmed target is not reached, the attempt declines to the unchanged
    route. Requested tolerances below \code{1.25e-12} retain the former
    rule with either setting.}
  \item{\code{XHDFE_MOBILITY_PROFILE}, \code{XHDFE_MOBILITY_MODE}}{path
    and mode (\code{off}|\code{read}|\code{write}|\code{auto}) of the
    mobility-diagnostics profile -- the R route to the Stata
    \code{mobilityprofile}/\code{mobfile()} options. Version-2 profiles
    distinguish ordinary from group/individual fits. Group/individual
    profiles require both domain-separated structural signatures to match;
    standard profiles retain their FE-signature contract. Legacy version-1 files and
    mismatched profiles are safe misses. In group/individual Auto, profiles
    cannot promote Gauss--Seidel or symmetric Gauss--Seidel: CPU retains
    joint LSMR and CUDA retains the grouped GPU solver. Profile write/auto
    does not benchmark sweep methods. A profile is written only after the
    requested route certifies; it suggests LSMR only after safe CPU LSMR and
    otherwise records Auto. Writing a profile (mode \code{write}, or
    \code{auto} without a matching profile) keeps the fit out of the
    reduced-system tier, as does a matching profile that suggests a
    Krylov or sparse solver. The group/individual gate uses
    the preconditioned operator \eqn{A=W^{1/2}DC}, weighted residuals,
    moments \eqn{CD'Wr}, and active normalized columns. Every right-hand
    side must pass either the consistent-system residual ratio or the
    least-squares optimality ratio.}
  \item{\code{XHDFE_ABSORPTION_CACHE},
    \code{XHDFE_ABSORPTION_CACHE_MODE}}{path and mode of the absorption
    cache that stores transformed outcome/regressors for reuse across
    identical specifications (Stata \code{absorptioncache()} /
    \code{absorptioncachemode()}). Both cache types accept \code{off},
    \code{read}, \code{write}, and \code{auto}. Mode \code{read} leaves
    the file untouched. Mode \code{auto} can refresh an invalid entry.}
  \item{\code{XHDFE_FE_STRUCTURE_CACHE},
    \code{XHDFE_FE_STRUCTURE_MODE}}{path and mode of the fixed-effect
    structure cache (normalized group ids and singleton filters) reused
    across runs with the same absorb structure (Stata
    \code{festructurecache}/\code{fescache()}/\code{fescachemode()}).
    Use separate files when combining the caches. Use \code{auto} or
    \code{write} to refresh older combined absorption entries.}
  \item{\code{XHDFE_FE_DIAG_FILE}}{appends per-run fixed-effect
    diagnostics (partial-residual statistics, fixed-effect totals,
    residual consistency, recovery method) to the given file.}
  \item{\code{XHDFE_PROFILE_SAVEFE}}{nonzero enables fixed-effect
    recovery timing traces on stderr (partial-residual construction,
    cached-hybrid reconstruction and checks, MAP-fallback stages);
    disabled by default with no output or behavior change when unset.}
  \item{\code{XHDFE_PROFILE_CPU}}{nonzero enables CPU-stage timing traces
    on stderr. Some reduced-system routes are ineligible with profiling on.}
  \item{\code{XHDFE_FE_NORMALIZE}}{normalization style of recovered
    fixed-effect contributions: \code{component} (default; per-component
    normalization) or \code{reghdfe} (aliases \code{mean},
    \code{mean_only}; reghdfe-style mean-only normalization).}
}
}

\section{Warnings and notes}{

\itemize{
  \item \emph{Saturated designs.} If the absorbed model fits the data
    perfectly (zero residual degrees of freedom), a note is emitted,
    \code{saturated} is set to 1, adjusted R-squared statistics are
    forced to 1, and standard errors are not identified. The reported
    coefficients are still the exact within-OLS solution.
  \item \emph{Multiway-cluster PSD adjustment.} If the multiway-cluster
    variance matrix requires the Cameron-Gelbach-Miller
    positive-semi-definite adjustment, a warning is printed (matching
    \code{reghdfe}) and \code{vcv_psd_fixed} is 1.
  \item \emph{Singletons.} \code{keep_singletons = TRUE} can bias
    inference when fixed effects are nested within clusters; see
    \url{http://scorreia.com/reghdfe/nested_within_cluster.pdf}.
  \item \emph{Group-mode singleton bookkeeping.} In
    \code{group}/\code{individual} mode the singleton counts are computed
    after the representative-row construction and can differ from
    \code{reghdfe}'s pre-collapse counts.
  \item \emph{Combined fixed effects.} \code{f1^f2} (like
    \code{reghdfe}'s canonicalized categorical \code{##}) creates only
    the joint fixed effect; list \code{f1 + f2 + f1^f2} for main effects
    plus interaction.
  \item \emph{Recovered fixed effects are contributions}, not group ids,
    and may not be separately identified in all designs.
  \item \emph{Non-convergence.} An exhausted iteration budget or failed
    numerical certificate raises an informative error without new estimates.
}
}

\examples{
## A small synthetic worker-firm panel ------------------------------------
set.seed(2026)
n <- 600
d <- data.frame(
  worker = sample(80, n, replace = TRUE),
  firm   = sample(30, n, replace = TRUE),
  year   = sample(2015:2020, n, replace = TRUE),
  x1     = rnorm(n),
  x2     = rnorm(n),
  w      = runif(n, 0.5, 1.5)
)
d$z  <- rnorm(n)                            # instrument
d$xe <- 0.6 * d$z + 0.3 * d$x1 + rnorm(n)   # endogenous regressor
d$y  <- 1 + 0.5 * d$x1 - 0.2 * d$x2 + 0.7 * d$xe +
  0.05 * d$worker + 0.03 * d$firm + 0.1 * (d$year - 2015) + rnorm(n)

## 1. One absorbed fixed effect (Stata: xhdfe y x1 x2, absorb(firm))
m1 <- xhdfe(y ~ x1 + x2 | firm, data = d)
summary(m1)

## 2. Clustered standard errors (Stata: ..., vce(cluster firm))
m2 <- xhdfe(y ~ x1 + x2 | firm + year, data = d, cluster = ~ firm)
## equivalent: vcov = ~ firm, cluster = "firm", or cluster = d$firm

## 3. Three-way fixed effects
m3 <- xhdfe(y ~ x1 + x2 | worker + firm + year, data = d)

## 4. Fixed-effect recovery (Stata: absorb(..., savefe))
m4 <- xhdfe(y ~ x1 + x2 | firm + year, data = d, save_fe = TRUE)
alpha <- fixef(m4)   # list: one full-length vector per dimension
head(alpha$firm)

## 5. Residuals and predictions (Stata: predict, xb / xbd / d / stdp)
head(residuals(m1))
head(predict(m1, type = "xb"))
head(predict(m1, type = "xbd"))   # xb + absorbed effects
head(predict(m1, type = "d"))     # absorbed effects only
head(predict(m1, type = "stdp"))  # SE of the linear prediction

## 6. Heterogeneous slopes (Stata: absorb(firm##c.x2 year))
m5 <- xhdfe(y ~ x1 | firm[x2] + year, data = d)
## slopes only, no firm intercepts (Stata: absorb(firm#c.x2 year)):
m6 <- xhdfe(y ~ x1 | firm[[x2]] + year, data = d)

## 7. Analytic weights (Stata: [aw = w])
m7 <- xhdfe(y ~ x1 + x2 | firm, data = d, weights = ~ w)

## 8. IV/2SLS (Stata: ..., endogenous(xe) instruments(z))
m8 <- xhdfe(y ~ x1 | firm + year | xe ~ z, data = d)

## 9. Combined fixed effect (Stata: absorb(firm#year))
m9 <- xhdfe(y ~ x1 + x2 | firm^year, data = d)

## 10. Group-level outcomes with individual fixed effects
## (Stata: xhdfe citations funding, absorb(inventor_id)
##         group(patent_id) individual(inventor_id))
pat <- data.frame(patent = rep(1:20, each = 2))
pat$inventor <- ((pat$patent +
  ave(pat$patent, pat$patent, FUN = seq_along)) \%\% 10) + 1
pat$year <- 2020 + pat$patent \%\% 3
pat$funding <- pat$patent \%\% 2
pat$citations <- 5 + 0.4 * pat$funding + pat$patent / 10
g1 <- xhdfe(citations ~ funding | inventor, data = pat,
            group = ~ patent, individual = ~ inventor)
g2 <- xhdfe(citations ~ funding | inventor, data = pat,
            group = ~ patent, individual = ~ inventor,
            aggregation = "sum")
g3 <- xhdfe(citations ~ funding | year, data = pat, group = ~ patent)

\donttest{
## 11. Side-by-side with fixest (coefficients agree to reghdfe-level
## precision under the default tolerance mode)
if (requireNamespace("fixest", quietly = TRUE)) {
  mf <- fixest::feols(y ~ x1 + x2 | firm + year, data = d)
  mx <- xhdfe(y ~ x1 + x2 | firm + year, data = d, cluster = ~ firm)
  print(cbind(fixest = coef(mf), xhdfe = coef(mx)[c("x1", "x2")]))
}
}

\dontrun{
## 12. CUDA GPU absorption (requires a CUDA build of the package; the
## request fails closed instead of silently falling back to the CPU)
mg <- xhdfe(y ~ x1 + x2 | worker + firm, data = d, backend = "cuda")
stopifnot(mg$gpu_used == 1, mg$gpu_status == "used")
}

}
